Manual review corrections for this page's first pass.

- Checked the "unit normal vector"/"normal line" exercises for the known
  bold-mathvariant converter gap seen throughout the Chapter 3 vector
  batch (r, T, N, etc. emitted as plain instead of \mathbf{...}). Verified
  against the live OpenStax page that the normal-line intro's "a vector
  n=<a,b,c>" is genuinely plain (non-bold) in the source MathML, unlike
  the curve chapter's position/tangent vectors -- so no \mathbf fix
  applies here.
- The same intro sentence had a comma misplaced inside a subscript --
  "$P_{0}(x_{0,}y_{0},z_{0})$" -- moved outside: "$P_{0}(x_{0},y_{0},z_{0})$".
- Three instances of a sentence-ending period and closing parenthesis
  trapped inside math mode: "$y.)$" (tangent-plane intro), "$ct.)$"
  (normal-line intro), "$\frac{da}{a}.)$" and "$\frac{dV}{V}.)$" (the two
  percentage-error recall notes). Moved the punctuation outside the math
  span in each case.
- Four "$N\,\text{to}\,M$" / "$N\%\,\text{and}\,M\%$" constructs (item 4's
  and item 12's endpoint changes, and item 13's two percent errors) fused
  an ad-hoc \text join with bare numeric math, the same pattern already
  corrected in sibling files' reviews; unwrapped to plain prose with each
  number in its own math span, e.g. "$10$ to $10.5$", "$4\%$ and $5\%$".
- The figure description before the AC/BC/AB items left every coordinate
  and expression as bare prose ("xyz plane", "(x0, y0, z0)", "z = f(x, y)",
  "df(x0, y0)") apart from an oddly split "$\Delta$x"/"$\Delta$y". Put the
  whole mathematical content in math mode with proper subscripts
  ($x_{0}$, $y_{0}$, $z_{0}$, joined $\Delta x$/$\Delta y$, and the labeled
  points $A$/$B$/$C$), and standardized "xyz plane"/"xy plane" to the
  hyphenated "$xyz$-plane"/"$xy$-plane" compound-noun form used elsewhere
  in this repo's reviews.
- All 34 point/vector-equation exercises were checked by direct
  substitution and are algebraically consistent with their stated points;
  no mathematical corrections were needed.
- One exercise ("$f(x,y)=e^{x}\cos y;P(0,0)$") used a semicolon to join
  the function to its evaluation point inside one math span, unlike every
  other point-exercise in this file, which uses either a comma inside one
  span or a comma-joined two-span form. Split into "$f(x,y)=e^{x}\cos y$,
  $P(0,0)$" to match the two-span convention used elsewhere in this same
  list.
